00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.160
Future meetings will determine

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.020
whether additional adjustments follow.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.600
Progress worth knowing comes from the city of Jingde-Gin

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.740
where porcelain sites have gained UNESCO status.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:43.220
The city has maintained porcelain techniques across centuries through generations of artisans.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.260
New artists continue the tradition alongside historic methods.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:52.160
Local authorities and artisans view the listing as support for preservation efforts.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:57.480
The process also highlights spiritual and cultural influences embedded in the work.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.960
International artists have joined local workshops to sustain the craft.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.820
The UNESCO decision underscores the site's role as a continuous center of

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:11.860
porcelain making. To really understand how sanctions legislation moves through

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:16.440
Congress begin with the fact that most reporting focuses on a single chamber

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:20.360
vote while the actual sequence requires multiple committees and coordination

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.140
between the House and Senate before any bill reaches the president. The House

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:29.860
can act quickly on measures that enjoy broad support. The Senate often requires

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:35.260
as unanimous consent or a supermajority to overcome procedural hurdles, which shapes which

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:36.320
provisions survive.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.460
Past sanctions packages on Russia have moved through both the House Foreign Affairs Committee

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.180
and the Senate Banking Committee.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.620
Members there negotiate carve-outs for specific industries or allies.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.580
Knowing this sequence shows why a bill can pass one chamber, yet stall or change form before

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.620
final enactment.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:06:00.200
Committee staff play a central role in drafting the precise language that determines enforcement

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:00.680
scope.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:06.360
Historical examples show that sanctions bills often incorporate input from the Treasury Department

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:07.540
before final passage.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:13.360
This layered review helps explain why timelines stretch even after an initial house vote.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:18.560
Readers contract the bill's progress by monitoring Senate leadership statements in the days

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:19.760
after house action.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:25.140
One concrete step is to check whether the bill includes waiver authority for the president

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:28.320
and how that authority has been used in earlier laws.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:34.540
If you want to go deeper on the sanctions package and rate decisions, compare how AP and Reuters

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.060
are covering the same events.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.420
Tomorrow, watch for whether Senate leaders' signal support or raise objections on particular

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.420
sections of the Russia sanctions bill.

